A||||9||88formula||700 TBI fuel pressure||||14||
Z||000000||88formula||03-01-2000||05:04 PM||||On the fuel pressure regulator for the throttle body on the 87 four banger, which way do you turn it to increase fuel pressure (clockwise/counterclockwise). I think it is clockwise, but I'm not sure.||||reg||14
Z||000001||Ozzy||03-02-2000||12:25 AM||||I did this to my '88 "Duck". I believe it was clockwise but I only determined that by looking at the way the fuel enters and leaves the throttle body. I think I figured that clockwise put more pressure on the diaphram placing more pressure on the plate causing lees fuel to pass into the return line. This increases pressure at the injector.<P>That was the theory anyway. I could be wrong!<P>Did not really notice any improvment in performance to be honest.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||03-02-2000||09:45 PM||||You do if your head flows more air, thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaveL||03-03-2000||11:33 AM||||What are you talking about, there must be something I missed. I have an 87 SE 2.5 DIS with about 130,000 on it. On the 700 TBI there is no provision for adjustment of the fuel pressure regulator. You can replace the diaphram, but thats it, as far as I know. The 700 is very easy to work on. I was having starting problems where I'd have to hold the pedal on the floor to get it to start. A new fuel injector cured this. It was so easy, I went out and bought the rest of the outside parts for it, replaced TPS, IAC valve, and fuel pressure regulator diaphram, all in less than an hour, with a minimum of cursing and almost no skin loss! ||||reg||5
Z||000004||88formula||03-03-2000||04:47 PM|||| If you drill out the plug in the fuel pressure regulator housing you can then adjust fuel pressure. I did some modifications to the head, which is why I am doing this. I will post what I did at a later time if you people want to know.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ozzy||03-03-2000||07:25 PM||||Yeah 88Formula. I would like 2 know how that porting job went. I'm toying with the V6 thing as you know but with a kid on the way and trying to buy a house, yada, yada, yada...need I blah, blah, blah more??<P>If I don't do the GT conversion thing I will still continue to improve the ol' "Iron Puke" (credit to SCCA Fiero for this lil' gem)<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||03-03-2000||07:49 PM||||Unless you have some type of drivability problem or the ECM is reporting errors, likely a 44(lean mix), after a port job you do not need to fool with the TBI. The ECM can adjust for a pretty wide range of change.<P>Try resetting the ECM if you haven't already. That will clear the old learn table.<P>Even if you do need to adjust it you will need a fuel pressure gauge inserted in the supply line.||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||03-03-2000||10:42 PM||||I have the gauge but need to make a fitting for it. The computer will only compensate for the increased airflow while reading the O2 sensor. When you mash the throttle the computer pulses the injector at a fixed programmed rate, open loop operation, therefore it still gives it the same amount of fuel as before with more airflow now. To get the optimal air fuel ratio under acceleration with the computer in this mode you have to increase fuel pressure, otherwise it will become to lean and the computer will not know it because it is in open loop operation.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 03-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Phaeton||03-04-2000||04:47 AM||||A piece here, a piece there, eventually I will understand the what, when, where, why, and how of performance chips.  Probably not this year, but eventually.||||reg||6
Z||000009||theogre||03-04-2000||05:00 PM||||make sure you even get to WOT. push the pedal to the floor then inspect the TBI.<P>Don't make any changes untill the gauge is installled. Then only make very small ones.||||reg||1
